WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.240
I’ve spent about a week with the new Apple
Watch Series 5, and yes, the always-on display

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.900
is basically the only real-world difference
I’ve noticed, which is definitely a little

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.830
disappointing, but I’ve realized that it
actually adds a whole lot more to the Apple

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:13.030
Watch than people think.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:16.700
This is the same exact reason why we saw a
BUNCH of people comment that they’re finally

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.320
upgrading to the Series 5 from their older
Apple Watch like the Series 1, 2 or 3.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.830
I’ll explain this reason in a moment but
first, I want to get a few things out of the

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:25.830
way.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.220
If you’re currently using the Series 4,
I don’t think it’s worth upgrading to

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:32.640
the Series 5, unless the always-on display
is very, VERY important to you.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.739
And EVEN then… it makes a whole lot more
sense financially to persevere for another

00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:36.940
year with the Series 4.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:41.010
But what if you have an older model, or maybe
you’ve never owned an Apple Watch and you’re

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.800
finally considering the Series 5?

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.190
Well, you chose the perfect time because I
honestly believe that for the first time ever,

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.940
the overall smartwatch package of the Apple
Watch is finally complete with the Series

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.889
5, and that’s why we’re seeing so many
people upgrade.

00:00:53.889 --> 00:00:57.670
The always-on display was the last piece of
the puzzle, which was my biggest complaint

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:58.670
about the Apple Watch.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:03.420
Apple not only added this feature, but they
flawlessly executed it, with new display technology

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.100
that allows the refresh rate to dynamically
lower down to just 1 frame a second instead

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.789
of the regular 60hz.

00:01:09.789 --> 00:01:13.670
However, I have a MAJOR complaint about the
always-on display, which I’ll get to in

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:14.670
a minute.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.759
Last year’s Series 4 brought some major
improvements to the Apple Watch, which help

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:21.250
the Series 5 be so far ahead of any other
smartwatch on the market.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:25.189
The most important changes were the new S4
processor, which helped the UI become super

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:29.350
smooth and lag-free, alongside the new larger
curved display that is just so much better

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:34.810
than before, a completely redesigned sensor
housing with new updated sensors, a louder

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:41.030
speaker, Bluetooth 5, the ECG sensor, and
now with the new model, a large 32GB of storage.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.939
This goes to show that the Series 5 now packs
basically all of the hardware you could ever

00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:49.991
ask for in a smartwatch, alongside some awesome
software improvements with watchOS 6, and

00:01:49.991 --> 00:01:51.659
people are starting to realize it.

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:55.439
There were quite a few users of older Apple
Watch models who were waiting for the perfect

00:01:55.439 --> 00:01:57.280
time to upgrade, and this is it.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.810
And now, let me explain what disappoints me
about the new always-on display.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:05.210
Yes, Apple did execute the feature very well
by dimming each and every watch face in its

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:09.330
own unique way to help achieve the same 18
hours of battery life while still allowing

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.660
you to see some important info on your watch,
but I have one complaint about it.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.800
I charge my Apple Watch every single night,
and I usually have about 40% battery life

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.470
at the end of the day, so I’m a bit disappointed
that Apple doesn’t let you customize the

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:24.510
always-on display to make it just a bit brighter
or maybe even keep the solid colors of the

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.830
watch faces instead of switching them to black.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:29.640
This would definitely use more battery life,
but I’d rather lose some efficiency and

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:30.970
end up having closer to 10% battery life at
the end of the day instead of having the always-on

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:35.190
display be so grey and dim, but let me know
if you'd like to have this option down in

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.190
the comments below.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:39.110
And there’s one thing missing that I think
would make a world of difference for many

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:42.460
people, including myself, and that’s a Watch
Face store.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:46.790
Apple does have some pretty cool new watch
faces with watchOS 6, like the California,

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:52.230
Meridian, and Gradient, but I would gladly
paying $10-15 for a customized great-looking

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.740
watch face created by a skilled developer.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.760
This way, developers would be encouraged to
create the best Watch Faces imaginable so

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:02.380
they could pull in some extra revenue, and
of course, Apple would take a cut of the sales.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:06.470
That’s a win for Apple, a win for developers,
and a win for us consumers.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:10.590
But as far as the Apple Watch Series 5, there’s
never been a better time to buy or upgrade.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.520
The battery life is great, the display is
bright, sharp, and now always-on, and its

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.100
jam-packed full of all of the features most
people need in their daily lives.

